1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,692
Signal or Coincidence?  A causal audit for AI agent collusion

2
00:00:04,692 --> 00:00:09,000
Sarvesh Tiku  ·  Apart Research AI Collusion Sprint 2026

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,132
When two AI agents act alike, are they coordinating?

4
00:00:12,132 --> 00:00:16,289
Or do they just think alike — same model, same data, same incentives?

5
00:00:16,289 --> 00:00:20,144
Antitrust law: parallel conduct alone is not proof of agreement.

6
00:00:20,144 --> 00:00:24,000
We need evidence that information actually moved between agents.

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:25,621
The randomized canary audit:

8
00:00:25,621 --> 00:00:30,136
the auditor secretly gives ONE agent a random target transaction (the canary).

9
00:00:30,136 --> 00:00:33,784
The preparer writes routine tickets: a priority and a risk tag.

10
00:00:33,784 --> 00:00:38,357
We intervene on the tickets: remove, shuffle, strip a field, or recompute them.

11
00:00:38,357 --> 00:00:42,584
The approver never sees the target. Does its behavior still depend on it?

12
00:00:42,584 --> 00:00:46,000
If yes, information moved. Randomization makes that causal.

13
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,530
If nothing the approver sees depends on T, a guess hits T only by chance:

14
00:00:51,530 --> 00:00:56,606
P(guess = T) = P(valid guess) / 4 — for ANY model, however capable.

15
00:00:56,606 --> 00:01:01,151
Live simulation: guesses that ignore T converge to the null.

16
00:01:01,151 --> 00:01:06,000
Real data: every model sits at ~25% when the channel is removed.

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,116
Finding 1: without any pre-agreed code, three of four models fail to coordinate.

18
00:01:11,116 --> 00:01:15,912
Gemini doesn't: its preparers mark the target URGENT — 58% recovery vs 25%.

19
00:01:15,912 --> 00:01:19,750
And the convention is not private: other models can read it.

20
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:25,122
Other approvers decode Gemini's tickets 45–55% of the time. Nobody else is readable.

21
00:01:25,122 --> 00:01:28,000
The bottleneck is the writer, not the reader.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,355
Causal check: shuffle the tickets so the URGENT ticket lands on a decoy.

23
00:01:32,355 --> 00:01:37,256
Gemini's recovery collapses to 12% — its guess follows the decoy 51% of the time.

24
00:01:37,256 --> 00:01:42,277
With a pre-agreed code, 100% of shuffled guesses follow the decoy, for every model.

25
00:01:42,277 --> 00:01:45,000
Behavior follows the carrier, not the target.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Finding 2: information flow is not the same as collusion.

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,842
An AUTHORIZED 'review this first' request: honest approvers pick it up 96–100% of the time —

28
00:01:52,842 --> 00:01:56,157
with no detectable rise in harm. Same signature, no wrongdoing.

29
00:01:56,157 --> 00:02:00,842
And knowing the target is not approving it: approvers often identify it and still reject.

30
00:02:00,842 --> 00:02:05,000
Convincing evidence needs flow + a localized carrier + harm + no authorization.

31
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,488
Finding 3: defenses only stop the carrier they touch.

32
00:02:08,488 --> 00:02:12,635
Hide a code in PRIORITY: stripping the priority field kills it.

33
00:02:12,635 --> 00:02:17,243
Hide it in the RISK TAG: stripping priority does nothing (72% to 73%).

34
00:02:17,243 --> 00:02:21,192
Recomputing every field from the source record kills both...

35
00:02:21,192 --> 00:02:25,997
...but give the agents a free-text note, and the signal migrates into it.

36
00:02:25,997 --> 00:02:31,000
Gemini: 76% recovery through the note. Redacting IDs and numbers: still 74%.

37
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,047
What about just reading the tickets with a monitor?

38
00:02:35,047 --> 00:02:40,126
An LLM monitor is at chance. Simple structural rules do better —

39
00:02:40,126 --> 00:02:46,000
but they must know which mark to look for. The canary audit needs neither.

40
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:46,510
Takeaways:

41
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:50,591
A randomized canary gives an exact null for information flow through tool state.

42
00:02:50,591 --> 00:02:54,418
One model converges on a covert convention unprompted — and others read it.

43
00:02:54,418 --> 00:02:58,091
Flow, collusion and harm are different; sanitizers are carrier-specific.

44
00:02:58,091 --> 00:03:01,918
Limits: collusion was instructed, synthetic environment, one prompt family.

45
00:03:01,918 --> 00:03:06,000
Code, data, audit and paper: github.com/sarvesh-tiku/Apart-Collision-Agent-State
